\\u003cextra\\u003e\\u003c\\u002fextra\\u003e\",\"marker\":{\"color\":\"#1baf7a\",\"line\":{\"color\":\"#fcfcfb\",\"width\":2},\"size\":9},\"mode\":\"markers\",\"name\":\"Connector forward (champion)\",\"showlegend\":false,\"x\":[2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025,2026],\"y\":[0.0207486941381311,0.328111011638317,0.3132618593038627,0.0249774300331026,0.5585651537335286,0.2362181818181817,0.3352389328642627,0.2281673713369296,0.4042334264295164,0.0539673278879813,0.3544989027066569,0.1492842535787321,0.2191633343586588,0.1906620021528525,0.085641088203001,0.0,0.1011567248809253,0.2608794197642792],\"type\":\"scatter\"}],                        {\"template\":{\"layout\":{\"font\":{\"color\":\"#0b0b0b\",\"family\":\"system-ui, -apple-system, Segoe UI, sans-serif\",\"size\":13},\"hovermode\":\"x unified\",\"legend\":{\"orientation\":\"h\",\"x\":0,\"y\":1.08},\"margin\":{\"b\":10,\"l\":10,\"r\":10,\"t\":40},\"paper_bgcolor\":\"rgba(0,0,0,0)\",\"plot_bgcolor\":\"rgba(0,0,0,0)\",\"xaxis\":{\"automargin\":true,\"gridcolor\":\"#e4e3df\",\"linecolor\":\"rgba(0,0,0,0)\",\"zeroline\":false},\"yaxis\":{\"automargin\":true,\"gridcolor\":\"#e4e3df\",\"zeroline\":false}}},\"yaxis\":{\"tickformat\":\".0%\",\"title\":{\"text\":\"share of team minutes\"}},\"legend\":{\"y\":1.16},\"height\":400,\"hovermode\":\"closest\"},                        {\"displaylogo\": false, \"responsive\": true}                    )                };                            </script>        </div>\n```\n\n\n**The trend:**\n\n- **Rim-running big:** league share fell by 5.2 points from the first to the last three seasons. Champions were 3.9 points below the league on average.\n- **Spot-up 3-and-D wing:** league share rose by 4.4 points from the first to the last three seasons. Champions were 3.6 points above the league on average.\n- **Connector forward:** league share fell by 4.1 points from the first to the last three seasons. Champions were 1.8 points above the league on average.\n\n\n\n**How to read this with the title results:** when champions sit above the line *before* the league's share rises,\nthe winners were early adopters of that role. It's a pattern, not proof that the role caused the titles; the\nChampionships and Impact pages test what actually predicts winning (player quality and availability matter more\nthan the role mix itself).\n\n## How trustworthy are the labels?\n\nThe checks the rest of the analysis relies on.\n\n\n| check                                                                     | result     | how to read                                               |\n|:--------------------------------------------------------------------------|:-----------|:----------------------------------------------------------|\n| Labels are stable when the data is resampled                              | 0.95       | 1 = identical groups every time; above 0.9 is very stable |\n| NBA players keep their archetype next season (same team)                  | 80%        | roles persist but can evolve                              |\n| WNBA players keep their archetype next season (same team)                 | 76%        |                                                           |\n| Agreement with blind hand labels, NBA (labels never used in tuning)       | 55%        | 6 archetypes: chance is about 17%                         |\n| Agreement with blind hand labels, WNBA 2025-26 batch                      | 60%        |                                                           |\n| Trades change roles: archetype change after a trade vs same team (NBA)    | 28% vs 17% | e.g. Bridges changed role, Dončić did not                 |\n| Defense check: low-usage rim protectors contest more 2s (NBA hustle data) | +1.8 SD    | independent tracking data agrees with the label           |\n\n\n\n",
